From b4ea1ae11f6df13ad1b78be8e52b8785e1aad5ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 23:42:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: tagged FFI objects: an odd handle is an immediate,
 not counted

`#[ffi(rust = "path", tagged)]` declares an opaque record whose handle may
also be an odd word that is not a pointer: an immediate the foreign side
encodes in the handle itself (lean2rr's one-word `Nat`/`Int`: a small value
is `(v << 1) | 1`, a big one a pointer to a counted big number, as Lean's
runtime boxes scalars).

The flag travels with the record (semi `Record::ffi_tagged`, HIR
`{ ffi tagged "path" }`, MIR `RecordLayout::Opaque { tagged }` printed as
`{ "path", @hook, tagged }`) to the dialect type
`!reussir.ffi_object<"path", @hook, tagged = true>` (a default-valued
parameter; the C API and the Rust bindings take it). In the LLVM lowering,
`rc.inc` of a tagged object updates the count only when the handle's low
bit is clear, and `rc.dec` calls the cleanup hook only then; nothing is
loaded from the handle before the test. Real boxes are at least 4-aligned
(the `u32` count at offset 0), so the low bit is free. Untagged objects
lower as before.

The two other places that read the count of an arbitrary rc value skip
tagged objects: the optional nonlinear-FFI instrumentation, and the
unique-carrying recursion analysis (`rc.assume_unique` on a carried
argument). Otherwise an `ffi_object` handle is never read through: it
produces no reuse token, is not deferred by the drop glue, and reaches
foreign code only as its Rust type, which knows the encoding.

Tests: conversion/tagged_ffi_object.mlir (guarded inc/dec, unguarded for
an untagged object, the type's textual form),
conversion/instrument_nonlinear_ffi_tagged.mlir, frontend/ffi_tagged.rr
(the attribute through HIR, MIR and MLIR), HIR and MIR round-trip unit
tests.
